-- Seven shapes an `l4 state-graph` caption has to survive. Each of them drew a
-- caption that said something false in September 2026, and no golden could
-- have caught any of them: NO golden captures state-graph DOT output at all
-- (measured 2026-09-22: `grep -rl digraph jl4/examples --include=*.golden` is
-- empty), which is why the assertions live in jl4/tests-cli/Main.hs beside
-- this file.

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Chair    HAS name IS A STRING
    Director HAS name IS A STRING

DECLARE Act IS ONE OF
    pay     HAS amount IS A NUMBER
    deliver HAS who IS AN Actor
    `settle the account with` HAS who IS AN Actor, sum IS A NUMBER

theChair MEANS Chair OF "Ms Ng"
a MEANS Director OF "A"
b MEANS Director OF "B"
board MEANS LIST a, b
base MEANS 7

-- 1. An inner obligation with no WITHIN, no PROVIDED, no AFTER, no binder and
-- no quantifier. Its HENCE arm leaves the entry state of its OWN obligation,
-- so party, modal and act are the second copy of what the node says — and
-- nothing at all is left. Suppressing there drew a BLANK arrow, which says
-- less than the caption it replaced: the arrow MEANS the act being performed.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Act
`nothing new along the arm` MEANS
    PARTY theChair
    MUST pay 1
    WITHIN 3
    HENCE
        PARTY a
        MUST deliver (EXACTLY theChair)
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH
    LEST BREACH

-- 2. A pinned argument that is a COMPOUND expression. Printed without its
-- brackets it reads as `pay` applied to two arguments rather than to one sum,
-- and the EXACTLY the author wrote disappears.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Act
`pinned compound argument` MEANS
    PARTY theChair
    MUST pay (EXACTLY base + 1)
    WITHIN 3
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH

-- 3. A barrier carrying a deadline of its OWN, distinct from the act's. Two
-- clocks take the one red arm — a member missing 30, and the barrier not being
-- met by 10 — so a caption naming one of them names the wrong one half the
-- time.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Act
`two clocks on one arm` MEANS
    EVERY Director d IN board MUST deliver (EXACTLY d) WITHIN 30
        ONCE ALL HAVE WITHIN 10 HENCE FULFILLED

-- 4. An act whose NAME contains spaces, beside a pinned reference and a
-- binder. `settle the account with` is ONE name and the wrap may not break it
-- across two lines; `a` is PINNED, so only that director discharges the act;
-- `sum` is nowhere in scope, so it BINDS and any number discharges it.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Act
`a name with spaces beside a binder` MEANS
    PARTY theChair
    MUST `settle the account with` (EXACTLY a) sum
    WITHIN 3
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH

-- 5. Two obligations over ONE act pattern, told apart only by their windows.
-- The node has to carry the WITHIN or the two draw as the same string, which
-- is the promissory note's shape (jl4/examples/legal/promissory-note.l4:87,
-- :101, :111) in four lines.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Act
`one act two windows` MEANS
    PARTY theChair
    MUST pay 1
    WITHIN 3
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST
        PARTY theChair
        MUST pay 1
        WITHIN 9
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH

-- 6. Shape 1 with a BINDER in the inner act and nothing else. The binder
-- clause is not the node's, so it survives the suppression and the arrow is
-- not blank; what it says is the one thing the act text cannot — that ANY
-- amount discharges this.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Act
`a binder is what survives` MEANS
    PARTY theChair
    MUST pay 1
    WITHIN 3
    HENCE
        PARTY a
        MUST pay amount
        HENCE FULFILLED
        LEST BREACH
    LEST BREACH

-- 7. The counterpart to 6: the same act pattern, but `base` IS in scope, so R1
-- resolves it and only 7 discharges the obligation. The act prints identically
-- in both — `pay amount` and `pay base` — and the binder clause is the whole
-- of what tells a reader which is which.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Act
`pinned where six binds` MEANS
    PARTY theChair
    MUST pay base
    WITHIN 3
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH

-- 8. A caption carrying an ODD number of back quotes. The wrap keeps a
-- back-quoted L4 name whole, which means it has to find the closing quote;
-- until 2026-09-22 a quote with no closer glued everything after it into one
-- unbreakable token, so one stray back quote in a string literal silently
-- defeated the wrap for the rest of the line and the box grew with no
-- diagnostic.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Act
`an odd back quote in a guard` MEANS
    PARTY theChair
    MUST pay 1
    PROVIDED "one ` quote and several more words after it" EQUALS "never"
    WITHIN 3
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH
